      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "s2_j61_i39_col30_night_launch_over_the_sangre_de_cristo",
          "description": "Reference run: WRF 4.8 real-terrain simulation of data/eqwefic/notes/gwdo_real_reference_run.md (Last Chance Gulch d01, Colorado Rockies, 8.1 km, 150 x 180 x 50, dt = 45 s, gwd_opt = 1 with ELVMAX supplied by WPS util/compute_gwdo.py -- without it GWDO is silently off everywhere, FORTRAN_BUGS B17). Step 2 = 2012-06-25 12:01Z, global row j = 61 across the Sangre de Cristo range, tile column 30 of the dump that starts at i = 39. Drag is ON: ELVMAX = 1770 m and var = 54 m clear both orography gates, the launch level is kref = 5, and above it the column has 8 saturating levels and 24 no-wave-breaking levels as well as a flow-blocking layer, so a single column exercises the whole of this block. Its minimum saturation margin |2 sqrt(temc) - temc| is 0.37 at Ri = 0.68, comfortably away from the critical value. Reference build: the real64 (kind_phys = real64) replay of the same dumped row through kernels/gwdo_diag_driver of WRF fork branch earthsciml-instrumented-gwdo, with the six scalar constants g, cp, R_d, R_v, eps_1 and pi supplied at binary64 precision instead of WRF's real32 module values (gwdo_real/mkflat64.py). They are subroutine arguments, not internal parameters, so that is what a double-precision kernel reference means for this scheme, and it is exactly what lib/wrf_constants.esm carries; promoting them flips no branch on any of the 140 dumped rows and moves the outputs by a median 2.7e-8 of the row maximum. Column tolerances are ABSOLUTE, set to 1e-9 of the profile's own maximum rather than per cell, per FORTRAN_BUGS N85. Every assertion is on an algebraic observed at t = 0 and this document declares no ODE state, so the empty time_span is a single static evaluation.",
